{
  "dom_id": "step-q48999",
  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/2c735c35700b6cda3aeb608c97d36871eed46db48e9c77f9f118db6a8f09383a.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. For which of the following values of $x$ is $f(x)$ concave down *and* ${f}^{\u2032}(x)<0?$",
  "worked_solution": "The derivative ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ is negative when $f(x)$ is decreasing. From the given graph, we see that only $x=a$ and $x=e$ are on decreasing parts of the graph.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/8cf8b0b8ea68a7fcaac0456770dab60bf124721897e65a95701ec928730e388e.png)\n\nThe concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($\u2323$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($\u2322$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOf the points $x=a$ and $x=e,$ the graph is concave down ($\u2322$) at $x=e$ only.",
